Jokers always lie. Clowns always tell the truth.

A group of four, each of whom is a Joker or a Clown, make the following statements about each other:

- P says, "Q always lies";
- Q says, "R always lies";
- R says, "P always tells the truth";
- S says, "Exactly two of P, Q and R are Jokers".

How many of P, Q, R and S are Clowns?

You are provided with a $7\times7$ grid of squares and nine cards with arrows on them: six square cards (side 1) with a diagonal arrow, and three rectangular cards with arrows along their length: of lengths 1, 2 and 3 (width 1), as shown. Place the cards so that:

(i) the arrows form a continuous route starting at corner $A$ (bottom-left) of the grid and ending at corner $B$ (top-right);

(ii) all of the cards are used;

(iii) the cards do not overlap;

(iv) no part of a card lies outside the grid;

(v) the edges of each card are parallel to the lines of the grid.

(Cards may be turned to point in any direction.)

Seven year old Rudolph 'Blaze' Ingram ran 100 metres in 13.48 seconds. Suppose that:

- the runner who came second took $1\frac14$ times 13.48 seconds;
- the runner who came third took $1\frac12$ times 13.48 seconds;
- the runner who came fourth took $1\frac34$ times 13.48 seconds.

In this case, what is the total of the times taken by the four runners (in seconds)?

Fill in each square of the $4\times 4$ grid with a number from 1 to 16, using each number exactly once. Numbers at the left or top give the largest sum of two numbers in that row or column. Numbers at the right or bottom give the largest difference of two numbers in that row or column.

The clues in the figure are:

* top (largest sum in columns 1–4): 13, 28, 25, 23;
* left (largest sum in rows 1–4): 13, 28, (none), 23;
* right (largest difference in rows 1–4): 7, (none), 8, 8;
* bottom (largest difference in columns 1–4): 7, (none), 12, 12.

You do not need to prove that your answer is the only one possible; you merely need to find an answer that satisfies the constraints above.

Let $a, b, c, d$ be positive real numbers. Prove that $d$ is an integer if and only if there are positive real numbers $e, f$ satisfying
$$\left\lfloor \frac{\left\lfloor \frac{x + a}{b} \right\rfloor + c}{d} \right\rfloor = \left\lfloor \frac{x + e}{f} \right\rfloor$$
for all real numbers $x$. (For a real $y$, $\lfloor y\rfloor$ is the greatest integer less than or equal to $y$.)

Let $n > 1$ be an integer. Find, with proof, all sequences $x_1, x_2, \dots, x_{n-1}$ of positive integers with the following three properties:

(a) $x_1 < x_2 < \dots < x_{n-1}$;

(b) $x_i + x_{n-i} = 2n$ for all $i = 1, 2, \dots, n-1$;

(c) given any two indices $i$ and $j$ (not necessarily distinct) for which $x_i + x_j < 2n$, there is an index $k$ such that $x_i + x_j = x_k$.
